Types of cursors

 Cursor Description How used

 Implicit Created automatically by Oracle for every DML and single- SQL%ROWCOUNT , SQL%FOUND ,
 row SELECT INTO ; named SQL SQL%NOTFOUND after the statement

 Explicit Declared by programmer for a multi-row query DECLARE → OPEN → FETCH (loop) → CLOSE

 Parameterised Explicit cursor with parameters CURSOR c(p NUMBER) IS … WHERE
 dept=p; OPEN c(10);

 Cursor FOR Opens, fetches and closes automatically FOR r IN c LOOP … END LOOP;
 loop

 REF CURSOR Pointer variable to a query result determined at run time OPEN rc FOR SELECT …;
 (can be returned to client)

 FOR UPDATE Locks the fetched rows; update the current row UPDATE … WHERE CURRENT OF c;


 WORKED CURSOR EXAMPLES WITH TRACE
 Data: employees(dept 10): Asha 5000, Ravi 6000; dept 20: Meena 7000.

 -- (a) Explicit parameterised cursor with FOR loop
 DECLARE CURSOR c_emp(p_dept NUMBER) IS SELECT first_name, salary FROM employees WHERE
 department_id = p_dept;
 BEGIN FOR r IN c_emp(10) LOOP DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(r.first_name || ' ' || r.salary); END LOOP;
 END;
 -- output: Asha 5000 Ravi 6000 (cursor opened with p_dept=10, 2 rows fetched, %NOTFOUND
 true ⇒ loop ends, cursor closed)
 -- (b) Implicit cursor
 BEGIN UPDATE employees SET salary = salary * 1.10 WHERE department_id = 10;
 DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(SQL%ROWCOUNT || ' rows updated'); END;
 -- output: 2 rows updated (Asha 5500, Ravi 6600)




