Cursors

 DEFINITION
 A cursor is a temporary work area in memory holding the rows returned by a SQL statement so they can be processed
 row by row. Implicit (Oracle creates for every DML/SELECT INTO) vs explicit (declared by programmer for multi-row
 queries: DECLARE → OPEN → FETCH → CLOSE). Attributes: %FOUND, %NOTFOUND, %ROWCOUNT, %ISOPEN.

 DECLARE CURSOR c IS SELECT first_name, salary FROM employees WHERE department_id = 10;
 v_name employees.first_name%TYPE; v_sal employees.salary%TYPE;
 BEGIN OPEN c;
 LOOP FETCH c INTO v_name, v_sal; EXIT WHEN c%NOTFOUND;
 DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(v_name || ' ' || v_sal); END LOOP;
 CLOSE c; END;


Exception handling
Run-time errors raise exceptions; control jumps to the EXCEPTION section. Predefined: NO_DATA_FOUND (SELECT INTO
returns 0 rows), TOO_MANY_ROWS, ZERO_DIVIDE, DUP_VAL_ON_INDEX. User-defined: declare e_name EXCEPTION; →
 RAISE e_name; → handle WHEN e_name THEN . RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001,'msg') raises a custom error
(range −20000…−20999).


