 3. Example – money withdrawal (consistency in one unit).

 CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE withdraw_money (p_acc IN NUMBER, p_amt IN NUMBER) AS
 v_bal accounts.balance%TYPE;
 BEGIN
 SELECT balance INTO v_bal FROM accounts WHERE acc_no = p_acc FOR UPDATE;
 IF p_amt > v_bal THEN
 RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20010, 'Insufficient balance');
 END IF;
 UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - p_amt WHERE acc_no = p_acc;
 INSERT INTO txn_log VALUES (p_acc, -p_amt, SYSDATE);
 COMMIT;
 EXCEPTION
 WHEN OTHERS THEN ROLLBACK; RAISE;
 END;
 /

 4. How procedures improve data management and consistency.

 Benefit Explanation

 Centralised business rules every salary / balance change goes through one validated routine, not scattered application code

 Atomic transactions several statements run as one unit – all committed or all rolled back

 Fewer network calls one call replaces many SQL statements (better performance)

 Precompiled parsed and optimized once, execution plan reused

 Security users get EXECUTE privilege without access to the tables

 Reuse & maintenance change logic in one place; auditing/logging included

 5. Disadvantages. Vendor-specific (poor portability); harder to debug and version-control; too much logic in the DB
 loads the server.
 Conclusion: stored procedures encapsulate rules next to the data, giving consistent, secure and fast data management.

